    <item>
      <title>Scripting Reference</title>
      <link>https://decal.ocf.berkeley.edu/labs/scripting/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://decal.ocf.berkeley.edu/labs/scripting/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h1 id=&#34;scripting-with-the-bourne-again-shell-bash&#34;&gt;&#xA;  Scripting with the Bourne-Again Shell (Bash)&#xA;  &lt;a class=&#34;anchor&#34; href=&#34;#scripting-with-the-bourne-again-shell-bash&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;&lt;/h1&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;While most programmers are likely familiar with &lt;code&gt;bash&lt;/code&gt; in its popular capacity&#xA;as a command line interpreter, it is in fact a powerful and full-featured&#xA;programming language. Moreover, &lt;code&gt;bash&lt;/code&gt; has a uniquely qualified claim to the&#xA;title of &lt;em&gt;scripting&lt;/em&gt; language in that programs written in &lt;code&gt;bash&lt;/code&gt; are simply&#xA;series of shell commands which &lt;code&gt;bash&lt;/code&gt; reads off and executes line-by-line. Or,&#xA;conversely, one might say that &lt;code&gt;bash&lt;/code&gt; command line entries are simply short&#xA;one-line scripts. So really, you&amp;rsquo;ve been &lt;code&gt;bash&lt;/code&gt; scripting all along!&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
